A 59-year-old woman presented with 2 days of left orbital pain. Medical history was significant for hypertension controlled with lisinopril and several months of epiphora without prior dacryocystitis.

Examination revealed:- Visual acuity: 20/25 OD and 20/400 OS- Edematous, hyperemic left eyelids- Diffuse limitation in extraocular movements in the left eye- Normal intraocular pressure- Normal fundi on both sides

{'Imaging Studies': {'Initial CT with contrast': {'Title': 'Coronal Computed Tomography Scan', 'Description': 'Left-sided lacrimal sac abscess with postseptal fat stranding largely limited to the extraconal space, minimal sinus mucosal thickening, and opacification of both nasolacrimal ducts'}, 'Follow-up CT': {'Title': 'Repeat Contrast-Enhanced Computed Tomography', 'Description': 'Increased abscess size with globe compression. Minimal intraconal fat stranding, and unremarkable optic nerve appearance'}, 'MRI/MRA': {'Title': 'Magnetic Resonance Imaging and Angiography', 'Description': 'Patent ophthalmic artery; diffusion-weighted imaging showed acute injury to the entire orbital segment of the left optic nerve'}}, 'Laboratory Tests': {'Cultures': 'Initial cultures isolated gram-positive cocci in chains; later identified as pan-susceptible Streptococcus pyogenes'}}

A man in his 70s with no history of chronic or allergic rhinosinusitis presented to the emergency department with a headache for 2 weeks and vision loss in the right eye for 2 days. Three weeks prior, he had rhinorrhea and was diagnosed with COVID-19. He recovered from COVID-19 but developed a worsening right-sided headache 1 week later and right vision loss 2 days prior to presentation.

Right eye visual acuity was limited to hand-motion detection, with severe pain on ocular motion.

{'Imaging Studies': {'Maxillofacial CT': {'Findings': 'Opacification of all sinuses on the right with diffuse inflammation. Bony erosion of the medial orbital apex bone, with extension of inflammation into the apex', 'Images': {'Title': 'Contrast-Enhanced Computed Tomographic Scans', 'Description': 'Complete opacification of the (A) right frontal sinus, (B) right ethmoid sinus with erosion of the medial orbital apex bone, extension of inflammation into the apex, and an air-fluid level in the sphenoid sinus, and (C) right maxillary sinus'}}}, 'Laboratory Tests': {'Serum IgG4': '277 mg/dL (normal, <123 mg/dL)', 'Sinus Cultures': 'Positive for Streptococcus constellatus'}, 'Pathology': {'Findings': 'Dense infiltrate of IgG-containing plasma cells, with most being IgG4 positive', 'Images': {'Title': 'Histopathologic Images', 'Description': 'A, Dense band-like infiltrate of lymphocytes and plasma cells present beneath the epithelium. B, IgG stain demonstrates 50 to 100 plasma cells in many high-power fields. C, Most of the IgG-containing plasma cells were IgG4 positive'}}}

A 62-year-old woman with multiple sclerosis treated with fingolimod for 3 years presented with a tender nodule on her forehead, which had gradually grown over 3 weeks. She reported bumping her forehead on an air-conditioning unit several months prior. She denied fever, neck stiffness, and photophobia. She lived alone with a pet cat and spent minimal time outdoors. She had no recent exposure to systemic steroids.

- Neurological examination was at baseline- Eroded, crusted papule on the left forehead- No other lesions on full-body skin examination

{'Laboratory Tests': '- White blood cell count: 3.9/μL (normal 4000-1100/μL)- Lymphocyte count: 0.65/μL (normal 1000-5000/μL)- Absolute CD4 count: 56/μL (normal 560-1840/μL)- CD8 count: 121/μL (normal 260-1230/μL)- HIV test: Negative- Serum cryptococcal antigen: Negative- Blood cultures: Negative- Cerebrospinal fluid cryptococcal antigen: Negative- Cerebrospinal fluid cultures: Negative', 'Imaging Studies': '- Chest radiography: Negative', 'Pathology': '- Skin biopsy: Granulomatous inflammation composed of histiocytes, giant cells, and lymphocytes admixed with numerous narrow-budding yeasts with thick capsules- Tissue culture: Grew Cryptococcus neoformansImage Title: Cutaneous Cryptococcus Clinical and Histologic PhotographsImage Description: A) Eroded, crusted papule on the left forehead. B) Skin biopsy specimen showing granulomatous lymphohistiocytic inflammation surrounding thickly encapsulated yeasts'}

A man in his 60s presented with a 2-year history of gradual-onset right facial weakness leading to paralysis. He was initially treated with oral steroids. At presentation, he denied hearing loss, tinnitus, imbalance, or other cranial nerve deficits. He had no other significant medical history.

Patient had complete right facial paralysis and a nonpulsatile mass protruding through the inferior ear canal. Audiographic findings showed mild sensorineural loss at 4000 Hz, but were otherwise normal.

{'Imaging Studies': {'MRI': '- MRI scan showed a 5 × 10-mm irregular lesion in the right mastoid near the stylomastoid foramen that had heterogeneous enhancement on T1 with contrast.\n- Follow-up MRI (9 months post-op) showed heterogeneous signal in the stylomastoid foramen on T2 images', 'CT': 'Imaging showed expansion of the fallopian canal distal to the second genu of the facial nerve, erosion of the posterior external auditory canal, and expansion of the stylomastoid foramen'}, 'Pathology': {'Gross Examination': '2 × 1.5 × 0.5-cm tan to brown, rubbery specimen consisted of cartilage', 'Microscopic Examination': 'Pleomorphic hyperchromatic nuclei, staining positive for S100 and negative for KI-67. Low cellularity and low MIB-1 proliferation index', 'Image Description': 'Microscopic image showing increased numbers of atypical pleomorphic nuclei in a myxochondroid matrix. Hyperchromasia and binucleate forms were found although mitoses were not'}}

An individual around 60 years old living in a rural area in the southeastern US was hospitalized with altered mental status, fever, flaccid paralysis of all extremities, and respiratory failure with lower lobe nodules and a left lower lobe infiltrate. Medical history included porphyria with polyneuropathy, alcohol use disorder, and a recent diagnosis of hyper-IgE syndrome, for which they received oral prednisone before admission.

Patient presented with:- Altered mental status- Fever- Flaccid paralysis of all extremities- Respiratory distress

{'Laboratory Tests': 'Bronchoalveolar lavage revealed:- Aspergillus fumigatus- Candida spp- Pseudomonas aeruginosa', 'Pathological Findings': 'Autopsy findings:- Disseminated aspergillosis and candidiasis without ocular involvement- Multiple organ involvement including lungs, liver, pancreas, colon, appendix, and kidneys- Inflammatory infiltrates with lymphocytes, plasma cells, macrophages, and numerous eosinophils- Right eye: focal eosinophil-rich inflammatory infiltrate in posterior choroid with larval sections- Left eye: focal eosinophil-rich inflammatory infiltrate in posteronasal choroid', 'Imaging Studies': {'Image 1': {'Title': 'Posterotemporal Choroid of the Right Eye', 'Description': 'Focal eosinophil-rich inflammatory infiltrate with a cross-section of a possible degenerating larva'}, 'Image 2': {'Title': 'Posteronasal Choroid of the Right Eye', 'Description': 'Coiled, incompletely sectioned filariform larva within a medium-caliber artery'}}

A man in his late 60s presented with a 4-week history of a rapidly growing left caruncular lesion. Medical and ocular histories were unremarkable.

Ocular examination findings were normal except for an amelanotic, pedunculated mass with an ulcerated surface, arising from the caruncle. There was no evidence of PAM (primary acquired melanosis).

{'Histopathology': {'Gross Examination': 'The specimen measured 8 × 6 × 5 mm and had a 4-mm ulceration with necrotic tissue on its surface.', 'Microscopic Examination': 'Revealed a solid tumor with an inflamed crust on its surface, composed of large anaplastic cells with mitotic activity.', 'Immunohistochemistry': 'Showed positive reactivity to melanocytic markers HMB45, MITF2, and Melan-A. The Ki-67 proliferation marker showed a significant number of cycling cells.'}, 'Images': {'Figure A': 'Ulcerated, pedunculated, caruncular tumor', 'Figure B': 'Proteinaceous material on the ulcerated tumor surface (hematoxylin-eosin, original magnification ×25)', 'Figure C': 'Mitotically active tumor containing anaplastic epithelioid cells (hematoxylin-eosin, original magnification ×400)', 'Figure D': 'Melanoma cells showing immunoreactivity for Melan-A (hematoxylin-eosin, original magnification ×100)', 'Figure E': 'Nuclear staining for Ki-67 reveals a high proliferative index (hematoxylin-eosin, original magnification ×50)'}, 'Additional Tests': 'Sentinel lymph node biopsy findings were negative. Conjunctival and orbital biopsies revealed no tumor.'}

A woman in her 60s presented with a slowly growing subcutaneous node in the right axilla that she first noticed 10 years ago, with progressive infiltration of the overlying skin over the past year. She reported experiencing swelling in the right axilla during pregnancy over 20 years ago that disappeared with lactation, followed by mild episodic pain in her right axilla during menstrual cycles.

Solitary, indolent, erythematous plaque in the right axilla measuring 5 × 3 cm

{'Histopathology': {'Punch Biopsy': "Dermal tumor infiltrating the subcutaneous fat without epidermal involvement. Tumor cells diffusely dispersed between collagen bundles and lined up in an 'Indian file pattern' with basophilic nuclei and scant cytoplasm.", 'Immunohistochemistry': 'Positive for: pancytokeratin (AE1/AE3), cytokeratin 20, GCDFP15, estrogen receptor, and GATA3 antibodies. Negative for: synaptophysin, chromogranin, and S100-stainings.', 'Excision Findings': 'Multiple GATA3-positive and estrogen receptor–positive apocrine glands in close association to diffuse tumor cell infiltration', 'Lymph Node Analysis': '1 of the sentinel lymph nodes showed isolated cytokeratin8/18–positive tumor cells and MNF116-positive tumor cells'}, 'Imaging Studies': {'Staging': 'Chest, abdominal, and pelvic CT scans, cranial and breast MRI, mammography, and breast sonography revealed no evidence of primary or secondary tumors', 'Images': {'Figure A': 'Clinical photograph shows erythematous plaque in the right axilla measuring about 5 × 3 cm', 'Figure B': 'Histopathology from skin punch biopsy showing dense dermal infiltrates of atypical epithelioid tumor cells with basophilic nuclei dispersed between collagen fibers in an Indian file pattern', 'Figure C': 'Histopathology from complete tumor excision showing closely associated apocrine glands apart from diffuse infiltrates of tumor cells', 'Figure D': 'Immunohistochemistry demonstrating GATA3-positive apocrine glandular structures'}}}

A man in his 60s presented with an enlarging purpuric patch on his left lateral superior forehead and left anterior scalp, initially present for 2 weeks. Further discussion revealed the lesion first developed in March 2014 and was fairly stable until June 2014, when there was rapid growth and extension followed by central ulceration and bleeding.

Clinical examination revealed a 30 × 25-cm centrally eroded purpuric plaque with multifocal peripheral reddish-purple patches, papules, and macules involving:- Left anterior scalp with extension posteriorly on left parietal scalp- Superior aspect of left occipital scalp- Anteriorly on left forehead and left lateral cheek- Medially to right frontal and right parietal scalpNo evidence of lymphadenopathy in preauricular, cervical, occipital, submandibular or supraclavicular areas.

{'Biopsy & Histopathology': '- Dermis permeated by irregular, slitlike spaces containing erythrocytes and lined by plump oval cells- Cells had pale eosinophilic cytoplasm and hyperchromatic nuclei- Cells stained positively with CD31 and were negative for CK5- Strong immunopositivity for ADRB1, ADRB2, and ADRB3 receptors- Ki-67 proliferation marker analysis performed', 'Imaging Studies': '- Chest and abdominal CT scan with contrast (June 2014): No evidence of nodal, pulmonary, or liver metastases- Staging: T2N0M0'}

A woman in her 30s with no relevant medical history was referred for a progressive bilateral nasal airway obstruction.

Endoscopic examination found a voluminous, pulsatile, highly vascularized mass in both nasal cavities.

{'Imaging Studies': {'Endoscopic View': {'Title': 'Endoscopic View of the Right Nasal Cavity', 'Description': 'A hypervascular and pulsatile tumor occupies almost the entire right nasal fossa'}, 'CT Scan': {'Description': 'Enhanced CT scan showed a hypervascular tumor in the nasal cavity, on the left thyroid, the fifth rib, and the L4 vertebra'}, 'MRI': {'Description': 'Confirmed a hypervascular tumor with anterior skull base extension (contrast enhancement after gadolinium injection, intermediate T2 signal)'}, 'Radioiodine Scintigraphy': {'Description': 'Found considerable uptake regarding rib and vertebral lesions. The sinonasal tumor had low uptake, indicative of thyroid tissue with low iodine avidity'}}, 'Laboratory Tests': {'Serum Thyroglobulin': '4432 ng/mL (significantly elevated)', 'Biopsy Results': {'Costal Biopsy': 'Pointed to VTC metastasis', 'Sinonasal Tumor Biopsy': 'Confirmed VTC metastasis'}}}

A woman in her 50s presented with bilateral nasal obstruction, thick nasal discharge, and facial pain and pressure. Her condition was recalcitrant to 3 prior surgeries, immunotherapy, and medical therapy.

Initial post-procedure examination showed:- Binocular horizontal diplopia with left lateral gaze- Inability for the left eye to track beyond the midline- Left eye was not proptotic, ecchymotic, or tense- Bedside intraocular pressure measurements: 15 OD, 16 OS (normal)- National Institutes of Health stroke score of 1

{'Clinical Images': {'Figure 1': {'Title': 'Clinical Images', 'Description': 'Images showing left-sided abducens nerve palsy immediately following surgery and its resolution by postoperative day 1'}, 'Figure 2': {'Title': 'Illustrative Representations of the Proposed Mechanisms', 'Description': 'Diagrams showing different hypotheses for abducens nerve palsy mechanisms following sphenopalatine ganglion blockade'}}, 'Emergency Evaluation': 'Workup results were negative for a cerebrovascular accident'}

A 15-year-old boy with biopsy-proven Crohn disease. He was initially on infliximab therapy (5 mg/kg subcutaneous injection every 8 weeks) and later switched to adalimumab (40-mg/0.8-mL subcutaneous injection every 2 weeks) due to disease relapse.

- Bilateral scaly, thickened, and erythematous upper and lower eyelids- Both lower eyelids were ectropic- Visual acuity was 20/20 OU- Crusty plaques on the eyelid margins - Diffuse conjunctival hyperemia- Rest of the ophthalmic examination was unremarkable

{'Images': {'Title': 'Eyelid Manifestations Before and After Treatment', 'Description': 'A, Blepharitis and ectropion during TNF-α inhibitor treatment. B, Resolution after treatment discontinuation.'}}

A 43-year-old woman presented with abnormal vaginal bleeding. Family history was significant for prostate cancer in her father and paternal grandfather.

No physical examination findings were explicitly mentioned in the case report.

{'Pathology Tests': {'Initial Test': 'Dilation and curettage was performed', 'Immunohistochemistry': 'Tumor stained negative for MSH6 protein', 'Microsatellite Instability (MSI) Testing': 'MSI testing was negative', 'Genetic Testing': 'Germline mutation R1331X was detected in the MSH6 gene'}}

A 43-year-old man presented with chronic eye symptoms. His initial visual acuity was 20/20 OD and 20/30 OS. Over time, his vision deteriorated to 20/30 OD and counting fingers OS.

Slit lamp examination revealed:- Subconjunctival fibrosis- Symblepharon formation - Corneal neovascularizations- Conjunctival hyperemia

{'Biopsy': 'Conjunctival biopsy and immunofluorescent histology revealed an unspecific result', 'Images': {'Figure 1': {'Title': 'Right and Left Eyes Before Therapy', 'Description': 'Shows condition of both eyes prior to treatment'}, 'Figure 2': {'Title': 'Right and Left Eyes After Therapy', 'Description': 'Shows condition of both eyes after treatment, demonstrating reduction in conjunctival hyperemia and corneal vascularizations'}}

A woman in her late 50s with a history of well-controlled hypertension and hyperlipidemia. She had been on methotrexate and sulfasalazine treatment. Initially developed mild aphasia and confusion lasting for several hours. A month later, she developed sudden-onset right leg weakness, which resolved within 2 days. Two months later, she presented with mild headache and right facial droop.

Neurological examination revealed:- Paraphasic errors- Word-finding difficulty- Mild right nasolabial flattening- Bilateral upgoing plantars- No motor or sensory deficits

{'Laboratory Tests': '- Elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein level- High titer of cyclic citrullinated peptide antibody- CSF analysis:   - White blood cell count: 12/µL  - Red blood cell count: 1/µL  - Protein level: 0.055 g/dL  - Glucose level: 58 mg/dL  - 4 oligoclonal bands corresponding to serum sample- Negative results for:  - Rapid plasma reagin  - QuantiFERON  - HIV  - Antiphospholipid antibodies  - Antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody', 'Imaging Studies': '- MRI Brain:  Title: Brain Magnetic Resonance Imaging  Description: Extensive white matter changes with enhancement along the arachnoid surface in the interhemispheric fissure extending over the frontal lobes bilaterally. No acute infarct in diffusion-weighted imaging sequence.- MRA: No vascular abnormality- Chest CT: No pathology or pulmonary nodules', 'Pathological Tests': 'Brain biopsy showed:- Thick exudate in subarachnoid space- Outer portion: necrotic cells beneath arachnoid membrane- Inner portion: lymphocytes and plasma cells with occasional Russell bodies and eosinophils- Multinucleated giant cells between necrotic tissue and viable inflammatory cells- No evidence of vasculitis- Special staining negative for tuberculosis and fungal infection'}

A man in his 40s, weighing 109kg, with stage IV KRAS wild-type rectal adenocarcinoma diagnosed in November 2013. He had pulmonary and liver metastases. His treatment history included radiation therapy and multiple chemotherapy cycles. In December 2014, he was enrolled in trial E7208 at the Smilow Cancer Hospital at Yale-New Haven and was randomized to receive combination therapy every 2 weeks.

In early January 2015, the patient developed a single, well-defined, blanchable bright red plaque densely composed of telangiectasias on his lower right lateral shin, which grew to 4 cm. The lesion was not associated with pain or pruritus.

{'Histopathological Analysis': {'Findings': 'Discrete clusters of small-caliber vessels were observed, with superficial, small lobules of small-caliber vessels. The epidermis was normal.', 'Images': [{'Title': 'Clinical and Histological Features of Vascular Lesion', 'Description': 'Figure 1A shows a single, well-demarcated, blanchable red plaque, measuring 4 cm in diameter. The plaque breaks up into smaller punctate macules at the periphery.'}, {'Title': 'Histological Analysis', 'Description': 'Figure 1B and C show superficial, small lobules of small-caliber vessels (hematoxylin-eosin, original magnification ×4 and ×20)'}]}, 'Genetic Analysis': {'Method': "Paired whole-exome sequencing of affected tissue and patient's blood", 'Finding': 'KDR (VEGFR2) c.C2312G, p.T771R identified as the only somatic mutation'}}
